  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid level ridge will retrograde west into the southern Plains with \nmore of a northwesterly flow developing across the Ohio Valley. \nSurface front acrs the southern counties will push south of the area \nand stall out overnight as surface high pressure builds east across \nthe northern Great Lakes/southern Canada. Showers and storms across \nthe far southern counties will continue pushing south of the area \nthis morning. Have cancelled the flood watch with the heavy rain and \nflood threat ended. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nThere will be a brief respite tonight into Saturday morning. But the\nchance of showers and storms returns Saturday afternoon. The front\nmoving through today will stall just south of the area. The western\nend of that will lift on Saturday as an area of low pressure moves\neast while the eastern end will lag. This should result in the\nboundary stretching across the western part of the forecast area.\nThis may become a focus for diurnal convection to develop. But it is\nuncertain how well defined the boundary will be at that point which\nwill affect the low level forcing. Some guidance is suggesting that\nthe old boundary will be frontolysizing while a new boundary develops\nnear Lake Erie. \n\nAn active jet stream across the Great Lakes will bring disturbances\nthrough the region that will generate convective complexes. There is\nthe potential for an MCS to drop southeast into the region Saturday\nnight. Later convection may take a more easterly path, being aligned\nwith a boundary that will be slowly moving southward through the\nweekend. The more active zone of convection near the boundary will \nhave the potential to produce repeated rounds of showers and storms \nat any one location. Plus the return of high precipitable water and \ndeep warm cloud depths will lead to efficient warm rain processes \nagain. This mean locally heavy rain will be possible.\n\nThe front is forecast to be south of the area by Monday morning where\nit will stall and then become reoriented more northeast-southwest as\nwaves of weak low pressure travel along it. This is in response to\nthe approach of a robust mid level system that will close off over\nthe region towards mid week. So, there will be periods of showers and\npossibly some storms north of the boundary Monday into Tuesday. And\nthat may even linger into Wednesday across eastern counties. But it\nwill be cooler.",
